          "distinct": true,
          "id": "9455fbb1c88de5ee9ba8da78171642749634a2ac",
          "message": "feat(provider): ship LLM provider adapters as opt-in Go modules (#362)\n\n* feat(provider): ship LLM provider adapters as opt-in Go modules (#362)\n\nThe engine module ships port.LLMProvider and mockllm, but the real adapters\nlived in the root module's internal/adapter/, unimportable — so an engine\nembedder had to re-derive wire-format logic that has exactly one correct answer\n(Anthropic prompt-cache breakpoint placement, message ordering, stream\nassembly, usage accounting). Getting it wrong is silent and costly: misplaced\ncache breakpoints turn every turn of a long tool-calling loop into a\nfull-transcript reprocess at full input price.\n\nforced on every engine importer — but that argued against the *engine module*,\nnot against shipping them. Ship them as a provider/ subtree of opt-in Go\nsubmodules, so the dependency is opt-in by import (Jeremy's mechanism):\n\n- provider/anthropic   — native Anthropic Messages adapter (zero couplings)\n- provider/openai      — OpenAI Responses adapter\n- provider/openaichat  — OpenAI Chat Completions adapter\n- provider/ssefilter   — the shared SSE keepalive filter (openai + openaichat)\n\nEach carries its own go.mod (require engine + its SDK; openai/openaichat also\nrequire provider/ssefilter), so a consumer go gets exactly one provider and\ninherits only that SDK + the engine's tiny closure. llmresilience /\nprovidercatalog / openrouter / openaicompat stay root-side (a harness decorator\nand stdlib-only leaves, not wire-format providers). The engine module is\nuntouched.\n\nRegistration: go.work use, root go.mod require+replace, Taskfile second passes,\nci.yml per-module jobs + a provider-standalone GOWORK=off gate (scoped to\nssefilter + anthropic — openai/openaichat defer until provider/ssefilter is\ntagged), perf.yml cache keys, dependabot, depguard rules, release.yml\ntag-grammar doc (provider/<name>/v*). New ADR 0093 records the decision. The\n~30 internal/adapter/... doc citations across the frozen ADRs + living docs are\nrepointed to provider/<name>/ (docs/lint gate), and AGENTS.md / user-docs are\nupdated.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Juan Antonio Osorio <jaosorior@gmail.com>\n\n* ci(provider): subshell the standalone gate's per-module cd\n\nThe provider-standalone job's run blocks did 'cd provider/ssefilter && ...'\nthen 'cd provider/anthropic && ...' on separate lines of the SAME shell — the\nfirst cd persisted, so the second resolved to provider/ssefilter/provider/\nanthropic and failed. Wrap each in a subshell. Same gate logic; scoped to\nssefilter + anthropic until provider/ssefilter is tagged (ADR 0093).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Juan Antonio Osorio <jaosorior@gmail.com>\n\n* ci(provider): subshell all multi-line provider cd sequences + regen llms.txt\n\nThe build/test/lint/vet/vuln provider jobs each ran several 'cd provider/X && ...'\nlines in ONE shell — the first cd persisted, so every later line resolved under\nprovider/ssefilter/provider/... and failed. Subshell each (the same fix as the\nstandalone gate). Also regenerate llms.txt now that the scratch PLAN-362.md is\ngone (159 docs / 0 orphans).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Juan Antonio Osorio <jaosorior@gmail.com>\n\n* ci(provider): scope the standalone gate to ssefilter — the private-repo fetch wall\n\nanthropic/openai/openaichat each require another mecatl module (engine, and\nprovider/ssefilter for the openai-go family). mecatl is a private/INTERNAL repo,\nso a GOWORK=off build must fetch that dep from the origin — proxy.golang.org\ncan't serve a private module, and the job checks out with\npersist-credentials: false, so the fetch 401s (git ls-remote exit 128). The\nearlier 'deferred until ssefilter is tagged' framing was wrong: even anthropic\n(engine is a published v0.8.0 tag) cannot fetch it privately. Scope the\nprovider-standalone gate to ssefilter (the only self-contained provider) and\ndefer the rest until the repo is public or git credentials + GOPRIVATE are\nwired; correct ADR 0093 to record the real reason.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Juan Antonio Osorio <jaosorior@gmail.com>",
