          "id": "da8c4c640e84697de98e8ce35593966e41335afe",
          "message": "feat(openrouter): downstream-provider steering + routing echo (#495)\n\n* feat(openrouter): downstream-provider steering + routing echo (issue #480)\n\nOpenRouter is a meta-provider: one model id is served by several downstream\ninference providers. mecatl had no way to steer which downstream serves a\nmodel or to see which one actually served a request. This adds both, scoped\nto the openrouter registry entry only.\n\nSteering (config → request): a new operator-tier-only `openrouter:` settings\nsubtree carries a per-model `order:` + `allow_fallbacks:` sent as OpenRouter's\n`provider` request-body object. Strict-parsed, project-tier WARN-ignored (same\ngate as default_provider/allowlist/router), aliases resolved to concrete model\nids at fold, fail-soft validation. The openrouter entry alone passes\nopenai.WithProviderPreferences (model-keyed) + WithMetadataHeader(true) over\nthe shared extra channel, stamping the body key via option.WithJSONSet and the\nX-OpenRouter-Metadata header as per-request options — buildParams and the\nbyte-stable prompt-cache prefix untouched, and never leaked to a non-OpenRouter\nendpoint.\n\nEcho (response → client): the adapter parses the terminal response.completed\nraw JSON's openrouter_metadata (fail-empty; absent on a cache hit) and emits a\nnew port.ChunkProviderRoute, which the loop relays verbatim onto a new\nclient-visible session.EvProviderRoute (\"provider.route\") — a string\npassthrough, metadata-only, never recorded, never a diagnostics line. mecatui\nrenders a transient \"via <slug>\" footer status.\n\nport.LLMRequest stays provider-neutral (knobs are adapter Options, not request\nfields). Two exported engine constants Added (minor): ChunkProviderRoute,\nEvProviderRoute — api-compat snapshots regenerated + CHANGELOG noted.\n\nReview (spec + security + architecture + reuse panel) drove three follow-ups\nfolded into this change: alias-keyed routes resolve to concrete ids, the\nconfig/echo slug-namespace distinction (kebab request slug vs PascalCase display\nname) is documented, and a redundant mirror type was removed.\n\nTests: offline throughout — permconfig tier-gate + strict-parse, composition\nfold, adapter metadata parse + strict wire-injection (httptest), a loop-level\nchunk→event test, and an end-to-end app test through the REAL openai adapter\nagainst an httptest OpenRouter server (body key + header asserted for\nopenrouter, asserted ABSENT for the openai parity leg, cache-hit emits no event).\n\nADR 0104 records the decision; docs (usage, architecture, IMPLEMENTATION-NOTES,\ngenerated config reference + skeleton, user-docs) and the AGENTS.md gotcha\nupdated in the same pass.\n\nCo-Authored-By: mecatl <noreply@stacklok.com>\n\n* docs(openrouter): note hard-pin 404 + slug/display-name asymmetry (issue #480)\n\nLive verification against the OpenRouter API surfaced two behaviours worth\ndocumenting before undrafting:\n\n- allow_fallbacks: false is a HARD pin: if OpenRouter can't satisfy any\n  downstream in order for the model (out of policy on the account, unlisted,\n  or down), the request 404s with \"No endpoints found for <model>\" and the\n  turn errors — no silent fallback. Verified live on both the Responses API\n  and Chat Completions; a downstream can be listed healthy on a model's\n  endpoints yet unroutable on the account.\n\n- The echo is OpenRouter's DISPLAY name for the downstream (\"Google\"), not the\n  lowercase-kebab config slug sent in order (\"google-vertex\"). Two different\n  OpenRouter surfaces / vocabularies; we relay the display name verbatim and\n  deliberately do not map it back to a slug (a guessed slug can be wrong).\n  Documented so nobody writes a config→echo equality check.\n\nControl case proving the feature sound: order=[\"google-vertex\"]\nallow_fallbacks=false on anthropic/claude-sonnet-5 routed to \"Google\" with a\ncorrect provider.route echo. The anthropic-only 404 is an OpenRouter-side\nrouting condition, not our wire format or the model's age.\n\nCo-Authored-By: mecatl <noreply@stacklok.com>\n\n* feat(mecatui): show routed downstream provider in the header model segment (issue #480)\n\nThe provider.route echo previously surfaced only as a transient footer status\nper turn, so the routed downstream wasn't visible next to the model id the\noperator is looking at. Track the latest routed downstream on the Model\n(providerRoute, set on each ProviderRouteMsg) and append it to the header model\nsegment as \"/ <name>\" — e.g. \"Kimi K3/Google\".\n\nThe suffix is shown only once a route has actually been reported this session:\nit is absent before the first routed turn, and empty on a cache hit (OpenRouter\nstrips the metadata) and for any non-routed provider, so the bare model segment\nnever carries a stale or fabricated suffix. It rides the same width-shedding\nsegment as the model id, so no header layout math changes.\n\nTest: TestHeaderProviderRouteSuffix pins no-suffix-before-route, the\nModel/Google segment after a route, and updates on a subsequent route.\n\nCo-Authored-By: mecatl <noreply@stacklok.com>\n\n* fix(openrouter): address panel review findings\n\n* test(openrouter): fix stale option name in a test comment\n\nOzz's review-followup rename (ProviderPreferences/WithMetadataHeader →\nWithOpenRouterProviderPreferences/WithOpenRouterMetadata) left one stale\nreference in the TestOpenRouterRouteFor doc comment. Cosmetic only.\n\nCo-Authored-By: mecatl <noreply@stacklok.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: mecatl <noreply@stacklok.com>\nCo-authored-by: Juan Antonio Osorio <ozz@stacklok.com>",
