          "distinct": true,
          "id": "5243a20eb365b16655a6a6e99cde3562dec8be80",
          "message": "feat(redisstore): support authenticated TLS Redis connections (#374) (#659)\n\nRedis connections gain ACL credentials and verified TLS, with all secret\nvalues read from Kubernetes Secret-mounted files — never from flags,\nenvironment variables, or errors. Client construction, TLS assembly,\ntimeout defaults, and the connectivity ping delegate to the shared\ngithub.com/stacklok/toolhive-core/redis layer (already in the dependency\ngraph via authn/oidc; no new modules enter go.sum). See ADR 0233 (this PR\nconsolidates the original 0228/0229 draft pair into one accepted ADR).\n\nBREAKING: an address-only --redis-url is now rejected at Build. Plaintext\nRedis requires the explicit --redis-allow-plaintext opt-in, which the\ndisposable kustomize fixture in deploy/mecak8s/ now passes. Production\ndeployments use --redis-tls (system trust store) or --redis-tls-ca\n(private CA); any credential requires one of them.\n\nAlso adds the same opt-in to e2e/k8s/oidc_helpers_test.go's\nbaseAgentArgs() — the kind e2e suite's OIDC-patch/-restore helper, which\nreplaces the Deployment's args wholesale. Found by actually running\n`task e2e:k8s`: without it, every OIDC-patched or -restored pod\ncrash-loops on \"redis store: redisstore: plaintext Redis requires\nexplicit opt-in\" the moment this commit's fail-closed check lands, which\nwould otherwise silently break the (untouched) e2e suite for every PR\nbuilt on top of this one.\n\nClient-certificate (mTLS) authentication is not supported, because the\nshared layer exposes no client-certificate field. Tracked upstream at\nhttps://github.com/stacklok/toolhive-core/issues/240.\n\nThis is the authenticated, TLS Redis slice of #374; it does not implement\nthe snapshot-integrity MAC or event-log integrity work.\n\nCo-authored-by: mecatl <noreply@stacklok.com>",
