          "id": "de8df3647d9f0dd4310a2373fbc9993ffd8fa6b7",
          "message": "fix(jsonlstore): assert the store root's real normalization contract (#744)\n\nThe hole: two jsonlstore tests expected the store root to be\nsymlink-resolved. `normalizeStoreRoot` has only ever called\n`filepath.Abs` — absolute plus lexical Clean, no symlink resolution —\nand #673 changed no production line in this package; it edited test\nfiles only, running `filepath.EvalSymlinks` over the *expectations*.\nOn Linux `t.TempDir()` is already canonical so the two agree and CI\nstays green. On macOS `/var` is a symlink to `/private/var`, so\n`TestStoreNormalizesRootAndConfinesInventoryNames` and\n`TestSessionStorageContinuity_Scenario1_AtomicCrashRecovery/temporary\n_replacement_shares_destination_directory` both fail on `main`.\n\nThe fix: revert both expectations to the un-resolved path, which is\nwhat the store actually produces, and document the invariant on\n`normalizeStoreRoot` so a future macOS flake is not \"fixed\" by\nswapping in EvalSymlinks on the production side.\n\nThe invariant preserved: the store root is the path the operator\nconfigured, normalized lexically. Every resolver-derived path,\nflock identity and diagnostic hangs off it, so resolving symlinks\nthere would silently rewrite all of them.\n\nProof: the two tests above now pass on macOS and remain unchanged in\nmeaning on Linux — they assert `filepath.Abs` semantics directly.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
