          "id": "633aea91a39802aefc676ba8bccd2e02b6e527aa",
          "message": "fix(provider/openai): pair each reasoning blob with its own item id (#432)\n\nA Responses turn that interleaves reasoning with tool calls emits SEVERAL\nreasoning items, each carrying encrypted_content cryptographically bound to\nthat item's own rs_… id. The adapter emitted one ChunkReasoningItem per item\nand the loop folded them by CONCATENATING the blobs while keeping only the\nLAST id, so replay sent every blob under one id and the provider refused it:\n\n  invalid_encrypted_content: The encrypted content for item rs_… could not be\n  verified. Reason: Encrypted content item_id did not match the target item id.\n\nReplay is stateless full-history and the rejection is a permanent 400, so one\npoisoned message failed every later turn — the session went `failed` and\nstayed there. Delegation-heavy runs hit it most, but nothing on the path is\ndelegation-specific: any turn with two or more reasoning items poisons itself.\n\nFix the producer: pack the ordered (id, blob) list into the opaque\nMessage.Reasoning string, as provider/anthropic already does for its own\nmulti-unit reasoning, and replay one input item per entry under its own id.\nStructuring the blob is the adapter's business — provider-neutral means the\nENGINE never interprets it, not that the adapter may not give it a shape.\nPairing a blob with the wrong id becomes unrepresentable. Nothing outside\nprovider/openai moves: no engine API break, no snapshot migration, no proto\nfield. A pre-packing blob unpacks to the single (Reasoning, ReasoningItemID)\npair, so sessions already on disk replay byte-for-byte as before.\n\nReplay also has to preserve the ORIGINAL interleaving. A turn emits\nrs_a -> fc_1 -> rs_b -> fc_2 -> rs_c, and the rule is to pass prior output items\nback untouched; grouping all reasoning ahead of all calls — trivially the true\norder when a turn could only carry one reasoning item — rewrites the model's\ntrajectory once it can carry several. session.Message has no field ordering a\nblob against a tool call, so each envelope entry records how many calls preceded\nit and assistantItems weaves the two lists back together. The drain is\nunconditional: position is best-effort, never dropping a blob is not.\n\nFix the recovery too, for the sessions already poisoned: one pre-commit repair\nattempt per Stream, gated on a typed 400 + invalid_encrypted_content (or the\nverification phrasing, for gateways that drop the code), removing ONLY the\nreasoning envelopes. Visible history, tool calls/results, phase markers and\nfunction-call item ids all survive — stripping those would trade this 400 for\ntwo others (GPT-5.x stopping early on a phase-less preamble; \"Duplicate item\nfound with id fc_N\"). Whether a message carries an envelope is decided by the\nsame unpackReasoningItems the wire projection uses, so the repair and the\nrequest builder cannot disagree. A failed repair is terminal for that Stream\n(Retryable() == false, honoured by llmresilience ahead of its own classifier),\ncapping the call at two requests so a repair that fails on a transient 5xx\ncannot be replayed and spent twice.\n\nThe repair lives in provider/openai because that is the only layer that knows\nwhat the error means: Session.Recover is a provider-neutral state transition,\nand llmresilience's 400-is-permanent classification is correct as it stands.\n\nSee docs/adr/0101-openai-reasoning-multiplicity.md.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
