          "id": "a148bf962665e76749dfcf635f9aaa68d9b434f5",
          "message": "feat(server): add GetCompatibilityInfo, the feature registry, and --deployment-id (#823)\n\nAn SDK's first question is \"what may I do with this server?\" and the only way to\nask was to create a probe session and read the ServerCapabilities echo off\nCreateSessionResponse. GetCompatibilityInfo answers it directly: api_major, the\noperator-enabled capabilities, the build's feature identifiers, and an optional\ndeployment label. Authenticated like every other RPC, with a GET /v1/compatibility\npeer reading the SAME projection so the transports cannot disagree.\n\nSEPARATE FROM GetServerInfo, AND NOT AS A NAMING PREFERENCE. This scenario was\nwritten proposing that name. While the stack was open, ADR 0245 (safe build\ndiagnostics) shipped an RPC by that name for build identity behind an explicit\nprivacy boundary: its response \"must never carry ... capabilities, authentication\nor TLS details, arbitrary configuration\". capabilities and features are exactly\nwhat that excludes, so folding them in would have meant superseding a shipped\nprivacy contract to save one round trip. Overloading the name the other way is\nworse: one message answering both \"which build is this?\" and \"what may I do\nhere?\" has two audiences and two change cadences. Identity stays on\nGetServerInfo // /v1/info; negotiation lives here.\n\nNO BUILD IDENTITY IN THIS RESPONSE. An earlier revision carried build_version\nderived from debug.ReadBuildInfo(). ADR 0245's ldflags-stamped\ninternal/buildinfo.BuildID is the single build-identity mechanism, and a second\nfield derived a second way is the drift the single-source discipline exists to\nprevent.\n\nCAPABILITIES AND FEATURES ARE TWO VOCABULARIES. capabilities answers \"what has\nthis operator enabled?\" and moves with config; features answers \"what does this\nbuild implement?\" and moves on upgrade. Collapsing them makes a --no-bash\ndeployment indistinguishable from version skew — a client would reject a\ncorrectly-configured server as too old, or call an RPC this build never had.\nAC1.5 pins the independence.\n\nfeatures are OPEN STRINGS, not an enum: the EvNoProgress/StopBudget passthrough\ndiscipline. An unrecognised identifier is ignored by an older client, so\npublishing one is a minor release rather than a wire-compat event.\n\ncapabilities reuses the EXISTING composition-computed intersection verbatim\nrather than recomputing it — the AGENTS.md invariant that capability truth is ONE\nintersection. Media stays session-authoritative: the image/audio hint here is UI\nchrome and the per-session CreateSessionResponse echo remains the authority.\n\n--deployment-id is opaque, bounded, printable single-line, and empty by default.\nIt is NEVER inferred from hostname, pod name, or environment: infrastructure\ntopology is not something an authenticated caller is owed, and a label the\noperator did not choose is a leak with no consenting author.\n\nImplements Scenario 1 of docs/acceptance/sdk-server-enablers.md (AC1.1-AC1.7)\nper ADR 0248.\n\nRefs #821.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
