          "id": "02951ed8c96159b7c26fdafa7c3a842ee49e342e",
          "message": "feat(mecatui): merge queued follow-ups, edit-back, and auto-resume on transient errors (#228)\n\nCloses #228. The follow-up-prompt queue in the mecatui Bubble Tea client had three UX gaps that surfaced together when a run died on a transient upstream idle timeout (the queue paused, offering only send-verbatim or drop-everything):\n\n1. Merge always — multiple queued follow-ups now combine into one prompt (joined by a blank line) and drain in a single step, matching Claude Code.\n2. Edit-back — ↑ on an empty input line with a non-empty queue pulls the merged queued text back into the textarea (non-destructive); esc stays clear-all. Rebindable keyMap action wired into both running and idle key paths.\n3. Auto-resume transient errors — transient/retryable failures (idle/stalled stream, Unavailable/DeadlineExceeded/ResourceExhausted, 429, transient 5xx, shared message vocabulary) auto-fire the merged queue; hard errors, user cancel, max_consecutive_failures, and clean stream close still pause. Auto-resume fires exactly once (merged queue empties before submit). Bare server_error is deliberately excluded (OpenRouter reuses it for non-transient faults).\n\nClassification lives entirely in the client package (Transient bool on ResultMsg/StreamErrMsg); the ui reads only the boolean, preserving the proto-free ui boundary. Numeric HTTP status codes are digit-boundary-matched so a code embedded in a port/model id is not misclassified as transient.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Juan Osorio Rosales <juan.osorio@stacklok.com>",
