          "id": "532513aa0351ea4207b18182555538b5e8e3aabc",
          "message": "fix(mcp): steer + tolerate GLM's nested-args serialization in CallMcpWithQuery (#253)\n\nCallMcpWithQuery wraps the remote tool's arguments in a nested `args`\nobject. GLM-family models (e.g. z-ai/glm-5.2), whose native\n<arg_key>/<arg_value> tool-call format does not express nesting, could not\nreliably serialize that nested object: OpenRouter flattened it into a raw\nstring, mecatl passed it through verbatim (stream.go records the provider's\narguments verbatim), and the remote MCP server rejected it with the opaque\n`json: cannot unmarshal string into Go value of type map[string]interface {}`.\n\nThe namespaced remote tools work because they expose the remote schema flat;\nonly CallMcpWithQuery's generic `args` container forced the nesting the model\nchoked on. Two changes, keeping the object contract intact:\n\n- Description/schema: give the model a concrete nested example and state the\n  remote params go INSIDE `args` (not top-level, not a string). This is the\n  primary lever.\n- normalizeRemoteArgs: tolerant, args-scoped deser — pass a proper object\n  through; recover a JSON object that arrived encoded as a string; otherwise\n  return a clear, self-correcting error naming the expected shape instead of\n  the opaque remote 400. No provider-specific XML parser (that would couple\n  this provider-agnostic layer to one model's wire format).\n\nVerified live against z-ai/glm-5.2 over OpenRouter + a real github MCP server:\n6/6 runs — including the exact organic list-PRs -> 25KB fail-closed ->\nCallMcpWithQuery recovery flow from the bug report — now emit a proper nested\n`args` object and succeed. Unit tests cover the object / string-recovery /\nunrecoverable / absent arms.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
