          "id": "c676c204014417a5a31bc156a5c439b04b5642c8",
          "message": "fix(llmresilience): stop credential failures counting as provider failures (#652)\n\n* fix(llmresilience): stop credential failures counting as provider failures\n\nThe direct-mode bearer RoundTripper resolves an OIDC access token per request\nand, when the token source cannot produce one, returns the error without\ndialing the provider. net/http then wraps that error in *url.Error, which\ncarries Timeout()/Temporary() and so satisfies net.Error.\n\nBoth classifiers reach their network arm on it. So \"I could not mint a token\"\nwas read as \"the network flaked\": retried MaxAttempts times per turn, counted\ntoward the SHARED circuit breaker, and once five consecutive failures had\naccumulated, replaced by the breaker's own error. Since BreakerError carried\nonly RetryAfter, the single message that named the fix, re-run the login, was\ndiscarded by the breaker that same error had opened. What the user saw instead\nwas an opaque cooldown that reset every 30 seconds for as long as the\ncredential stayed broken, with no request ever reaching the gateway and\ntherefore nothing in its logs either.\n\nTwo changes:\n\nAn exported ErrCredentials sentinel, checked in DefaultClassifier and\nisTransientForBreaker BEFORE the transport arms, marks a failure to mint or\nload a credential. The bearer RoundTripper wraps its token-source errors with\nit. Such a failure is now permanent (replaying the request cannot mint a token)\nand breaker-neutral (it happens before the provider is dialed, so it is no\nevidence about provider health, and a shared breaker would take down every\nsession over one caller's token). The actionable cause reaches the caller on\nthe first attempt.\n\nBreakerError gains a Cause field carrying the failure that opened the breaker,\nrendered in the message and reachable through Unwrap. A breaker rejection is\notherwise a dead end for diagnosis: an operator sees a cooldown and never\nlearns what went wrong.\n\nThe regression test drives the real error shape, building the *url.Error\nwrapper through an actual http.Client rather than asserting against a\nhand-made error, since that wrapper is the whole reason the misclassification\nexisted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(llmresilience): keep breaker errors session-neutral\n\nCo-Authored-By: GPT-5.6 Terra <noreply@openai.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Jakub Hrozek <jakub@stacklok.com>\nCo-authored-by: GPT-5.6 Terra <noreply@openai.com>",
